Package: com.example.multifloat
Name: MultiFloat
Version: 0.12.0
Architecture: iphoneos-arm64e
Description: Multi-floating window / split-view tweak for iOS 17 (roothide / arm64e). 0.12.0 fixes the regression introduced by 0.10.0 and left unfixed in 0.11.0, where the floating window went black as soon as you left the app or returned to the home screen. The user's own comparison is the key datum: 0.9.1 was fine on this point - it flashed once while switching apps, but the floating window never went black - while 0.10.0 and 0.11.0 both go black. Archive 12 shows why, and the causal chain is clean in both rounds. In the Filza round the scene was already mounted with deactivationReasons=33 and lost its layers, 2 to 0, about one second later. In the Safari round everything was healthy until the home button was pressed; deactivationReasons then went to 32 while contentState and layers were still 2, and roughly one second later the layers dropped to 0. From that point on, writing settings is useless: that round contains 81 consecutive full writes of level, displayConfiguration, idleModeEnabled, deactivationReasons and frame, and the layer count never moved off zero. So a layer teardown is irreversible, and the only moment that can prevent it is the one second where deactivationReasons is non-zero while the layers are still present. 0.9.1 counted that state as requiring immediate action, which is exactly why it never lost the picture; 0.10.0 reclassified it as an advisory-only log line that must never trigger a write, so a black screen became unavoidable; and 0.11.0 kept that classification and merely made the reactive write heavier, which is why it did not help. 0.12.0 restores the preventive tier, but deliberately pays a much lower price than 0.9.1 did. The preventive write touches only idleModeEnabled and deactivationReasons, two pure state flags that change no geometry at all, and never touches level, displayConfiguration or the frame. Archive 10 established that the visible flash came from setDisplayConfiguration resetting the frame to the full screen together with the setFrame call that followed it, so a state-only write should not produce it. The tier is throttled by keepAlivePreventMs, default 250 ms, which is well under the roughly one second teardown deadline, and it can be switched off with keepAlivePrevent=false for A/B comparison. keepAlivePreventHard adds setLevel to the preventive write if clearing the state alone proves insufficient. The reactive full write is unchanged for a genuine drop, with backoff instead of giving up. Three further findings from archive 12 are addressed. First, the presentation-view size correction was firing up to twenty times a second and fighting the presenter's own layout, growing the log to 74 KB; it now has an 8 percent tolerance and a 500 ms throttle, while deviations above 25 percent still bypass the throttle. Second, MFSyncSceneGeometry is now re-entrancy guarded, because the crash attribution file pointed at step P7-geom, which was seen running three times in a row after S7-settle and could recurse through the layout pass. Third, a misleading log field was fixed: the dc value was printing the class of an already formatted string rather than the scene's display configuration, so it never actually revealed whether setDisplayConfiguration was being called at all.
Maintainer: QingC12 <QingC12@users.noreply.github.com>
Author: QingC12
Section: Tweaks
Depends: firmware (>= 15.0)
